/** @mainpage

This suite exercises tsf-usb - enumerating the USB devices attached to
the Test Agent it runs on, over libusb-1.0:

- @ref usb-list - take a snapshot of every attached device, log each
  (vendor/product, device and interface classes, strings), and read one
  device's full config/interface/endpoint descriptor tree back;
- @ref usb-audit - read the inventory as a security posture through
  tsf-cybersec and gate on it: a single device that is both a keyboard
  and a disk (the BadUSB composite), a firmware-update or vendor-opaque
  interface, storage or input where a policy forbids it, or a device off
  the allowlist.

It is read-only - the agent enumerates and reads descriptors, it never
configures a device or transfers data. What is attached is the host's to
decide, so the suite asserts no device count: a host with nothing
plugged in passes cleanly, and the audit fails only on a finding at
least HIGH. The point is that tsf-usb enumerates over the library (not
by scraping lsusb), parses the descriptors into structured devices, and
classifies them into findings.

*/
